In Java, an **interface** defines a contract that classes can implement.
Interviewers often ask about “types of interfaces” by usage. Common categories:

────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 1. Marker Interface (also called Tagging Interface)
Definition: Interface with **no methods** (and typically no fields).

Purpose: “Mark” a class so JVM/frameworks can treat it specially (via `instanceof` / type checks).

Examples:
- `Serializable` → object can be serialized
- `Cloneable` → class allows cloning behavior
- `Remote` (`java.rmi.Remote`) → marker that object can be used remotely

```java
class MyClass implements Serializable {
    // no methods required; just the mark
}
```

Note:
Tagging interface = same idea as marker interface (synonym).


────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 2. Functional Interface (SAM = Single Abstract Method)
Definition: Interface with **exactly one abstract method**.

Purpose: Used with lambdas and method references.

Annotation: `@FunctionalInterface` (optional, but recommended; compiler enforces SAM rule)

Important:
- May also have `default` methods and `static` methods
- Still functional if only **one abstract** method remains

Examples:
- `Runnable` → `run()`
- `Callable<V>` → `call()`
- `Comparator<T>` → `compare(...)`
- `Comparable<T>` → `compareTo(...)`  (yes, it is functional)
- `java.util.function.*` → `Function`, `Predicate`, `Consumer`, `Supplier`

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface MyFunctionalInterface {
    void performAction(); // only abstract method
}
```


────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 3. Normal / Regular Interface
Definition: General contract interface, usually with **multiple abstract methods**.

Examples: `List`, `Map`, `Set`, `Vehicle` (custom)

```java
interface Vehicle {
    void start();
    void stop();
}
```

Interview nuance:
If an interface has exactly one abstract method, it is both a normal contract and a functional interface.
“Normal” usually means “not marker / not specially SAM-focused.”


────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 4. Nested Interface
Interface declared inside a class or another interface.

```java
class Outer {
    interface InnerInterface {   // nested interface
        void display();
    }
}
```

Important Java rule:
Nested interfaces are **implicitly static**.
(There is no true non-static nested interface.)

Access example:
```java
Outer.InnerInterface obj = () -> System.out.println("hi");
```


────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 5. Remote Interface (RMI style)
Used in Java RMI (Remote Method Invocation) so a client can call methods on an object running in another JVM.

Pattern:
- Marker `java.rmi.Remote` has no methods
- Your service interface **extends Remote** and declares remote methods
- Each remote method typically throws `RemoteException`
- Implementation usually extends `UnicastRemoteObject`
- Server binds the object in RMI Registry; client looks it up and calls methods

```java
import java.rmi.Remote;
import java.rmi.RemoteException;
import java.rmi.registry.LocateRegistry;
import java.rmi.registry.Registry;
import java.rmi.server.UnicastRemoteObject;

// 1) Remote interface (shared contract — client + server both need this)
public interface Calculator extends Remote {
    int add(int a, int b) throws RemoteException;
    int subtract(int a, int b) throws RemoteException;
}

// 2) Remote implementation (runs on the SERVER)
public class CalculatorImpl extends UnicastRemoteObject implements Calculator {
    public CalculatorImpl() throws RemoteException {
        super();
    }

    @Override
    public int add(int a, int b) throws RemoteException {
        return a + b;
    }

    @Override
    public int subtract(int a, int b) throws RemoteException {
        return a - b;
    }
}

// 3) Server — creates object and registers it
public class CalculatorServer {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        CalculatorImpl calc = new CalculatorImpl();

        // Start registry on port 1099 (default RMI port)
        Registry registry = LocateRegistry.createRegistry(1099);

        // Bind remote object with a name clients will look up
        registry.rebind("CalculatorService", calc);

        System.out.println("Calculator server is ready...");
    }
}

// 4) Client — looks up remote object and calls methods
public class CalculatorClient {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // Connect to registry on server machine
        // Use "localhost" if same machine; otherwise use server IP e.g. "192.168.1.10"
        Registry registry = LocateRegistry.getRegistry("localhost", 1099);

        // Lookup by the same name used in rebind(...)
        Calculator calc = (Calculator) registry.lookup("CalculatorService");

        // These calls look local, but execute on the SERVER
        int sum = calc.add(10, 5);
        int diff = calc.subtract(10, 5);

        System.out.println("add(10, 5) = " + sum);        // 15
        System.out.println("subtract(10, 5) = " + diff);  // 5
    }
}
```

How it works:
1. Start `CalculatorServer` → object lives on server JVM and is registered as `"CalculatorService"`
2. Start `CalculatorClient` → looks up that name, gets a stub/proxy
3. `calc.add(10, 5)` on client → RMI sends call over network → `CalculatorImpl.add` runs on server → result returns to client

So:
- `Remote` itself = marker
- `Calculator` = remote interface (your RMI API)
- `CalculatorImpl` = server-side class that actually performs the work
- Client never runs `CalculatorImpl`; it only holds a stub and calls through `Calculator`

Same idea with Payment example:
- Server 1 = Payment service (has `PaymentServiceImpl` logic)
- Server 2 = your app (looks up by Server 1 IP and calls remote methods)
- Work runs on Server 1; Server 2 only gets results


────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 RMI vs REST (interview comparison)

Both let Server 2 call logic on Server 1. Difference is **how** they talk.

| Point              | RMI                                      | REST                                      |
| ------------------ | ---------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| Style              | Remote **method call** (looks like Java method) | Remote **HTTP API** (URL + HTTP verbs) |
| Protocol           | Java RMI / JRMP                          | HTTP (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE)             |
| Data format        | Java serialized objects                  | Usually JSON / XML                        |
| Language           | Mostly **Java-to-Java**                  | Any language (Java, JS, Python, etc.)     |
| Contract           | Java interface extending `Remote`        | URL paths + request/response body         |
| Discovery          | RMI Registry (`lookup("PaymentService")`) | Service URL (`http://server1/api/pay`)  |
| Coupling           | Tight (shared Java interface + stubs)    | Loose (only HTTP + JSON contract)         |
| Firewall / web     | Harder (special ports, Java-specific)    | Easy (standard HTTP/HTTPS ports)          |
| Modern usage       | Rare / legacy in new systems             | Very common for microservices             |

Payment example mentally:

```text
RMI:
  payment.pay(orderId, amount);   // Java method call over network

REST:
  POST http://server1:8080/api/payments
  Body: { "orderId": 101, "amount": 500 }
```

Interview takeaway:
- Same goal: call another server’s logic
- RMI = Java object/method style
- REST = HTTP + JSON style (preferred today for microservices)


────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 6. Common Java 8 built-in functional interfaces
Under `java.util.function`:

| Interface        | Abstract method idea        | Example use              |
| ---------------- | --------------------------- | ------------------------ |
| `Predicate<T>`   | `test(T)` → boolean         | filter conditions        |
| `Function<T,R>`  | `apply(T)` → R              | map/transform            |
| `Consumer<T>`    | `accept(T)` → void          | forEach / side-effects   |
| `Supplier<T>`    | `get()` → T                 | lazy create/provide value|
| `UnaryOperator<T>` | Function T→T              | same-type transform      |
| `BinaryOperator<T>`| apply(T,T)→T              | reduce-style ops         |


────────────────────────────────────────
🔹 Extra modern notes (good for interviews)
- Java 8+: `default` + `static` methods in interfaces
- Java 9+: `private` methods in interfaces (shared helper code)
- Java 17+: `sealed` interfaces (control which types may implement)


🔹 Summary Table
| Type                    | Key point                                      | Example                      |
| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Marker / Tagging        | No methods; marks capability                   | `Serializable`               |
| Functional (SAM)        | Exactly one abstract method                    | `Runnable`, `Function<T,R>`  |
| Normal / Regular        | Multi-method contract (typical APIs)           | `List`, `Map`                |
| Nested                  | Declared inside class/interface (implicitly static) | `Outer.InnerInterface`  |
| Remote (RMI)            | Extends `Remote`, methods throw `RemoteException` | `Calculator`              |


🎤 Quick interview traps
1) `Comparable` is a functional interface (one abstract method).
2) Marker ≠ Remote service interface; `Remote` is marker, your API methods are separate.
3) Functional interfaces can still have default/static methods.
4) Nested interfaces are implicitly static.
